    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of brush in direct contact with the structure and creating greater separation between shrub masses and building surfaces could meaningfully reduce fuel continuity and improve defensible space around the improvements. Where vegetation is growing immediately adjacent to exterior walls, foundations, or under eaves, thinning or removing that material would reduce the opportunity for surface fire to reach the structure. Managing the heavier ground vegetation and creating horizontal spacing between remaining shrub clusters could interrupt continuous fuel pathways across the property. Along the driveway, maintaining cleared margins on both sides would support firefighter access and improve evacuation visibility given the length of the approach route. Addressing areas where mixed and dense shrub cover transitions close to the structure perimeter, and maintaining separation between the tree cluster and adjacent shrub masses below, could further reduce vertical fuel continuity and support suppression operations over the long term."
